{
  "evidence": "TEXT_C: Bill of Rights guaranteeing civil, social, and economic rights (free healthcare, housing, education, decent work, minimum wage); increase child benefit, restore 16–17 benefits, pension rises, end means-testing for residential elderly care; free nursery education for all 3–4-year-olds and restored student grants; build up to 20,000 affordable homes and protect new social housing from right-to-buy; integrate and fund the NHS (+£35m annually), ban tobacco advertising, raise tobacco duty, improve nutrition standards; adopt EU Social Chapter, introduce a minimum wage, strengthen trade union rights; stricter environmental and pollution controls. TEXT_F: Tight control of public spending and low taxes; welfare-to-work expansion and strong anti–benefit-fraud measures; expand competition/privatisation (rail, London Underground, air traffic services); tougher crime measures (minimum sentences, end automatic early release, CCTV, curfews), school discipline, inspections and selection; maintain UK constitutional integrity, NATO centrality, and EU opt-outs; NHS spending to rise but with market mechanisms (GP fundholding, private finance).",
  "reasoning": "Because Care and Equality are the electorate’s top priorities, TEXT_C’s social rights, expanded benefits, NHS integration/funding, affordable housing, minimum wage, and environmental health protections align more directly with reducing harm and promoting equal treatment/outcomes. While the electorate has some value for Authority and Loyalty, TEXT_F emphasizes these (and proportionality/market competition) far more than Care/Equality, making it a weaker fit overall.",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "winner": "TEXT_C"
}
